      <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>
          Automotive manufacturing chemistry involves complex interactions between different chemical classes and processing stages. The questions and answers below address common queries regarding performance parameters, selection criteria, and processing requirements for coatings, adhesives, and lubricants used in vehicle assembly.
        </p>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the primary role of cathodic electrodeposition (e-coat) in automotive manufacturing chemistry?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Cathodic electrodeposition (e-coat) serves as the primary corrosion protection layer applied to the assembled vehicle body. The steel frame is immersed in an aqueous bath of charged epoxy-amine resins and pigment dispersions. Direct electric current is applied, causing the polymer particles to migrate and deposit uniformly onto the cathodic metal surface, including interior box sections and hidden crevices. The resulting film is subsequently baked to crosslink into a dense, chemically resistant thermoset barrier that protects the substrate from environmental moisture and oxygen.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How do structural adhesives improve vehicle crash safety compared to traditional spot welding?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Traditional spot welding concentrates stress at localized thermal joint points, which can fail under severe crash impact. In contrast, automotive structural adhesives (primarily toughened 2K epoxies or MMAs) distribute dynamic loads uniformly across the entire bonded joint area. This eliminates high-stress concentrations, increases the torsional rigidity of the body-in-white frame, and absorbs kinetic energy during impact, measurably improving vehicle crashworthiness and occupant safety.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What are the chemical differences between metalworking fluids used in stamping versus precision machining?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Metalworking fluids for metal stamping require high boundary lubrication properties to prevent metal-on-metal friction under extreme stamping pressure; they are typically formulated with high concentrations of mineral base oils and extreme pressure (EP) additives (esters, sulfonates). Precision machining fluids, conversely, must prioritize cooling and chip evacuation, relying on water-miscible soluble oils, semi-synthetics, or synthetic fluids containing wetting agents, corrosion inhibitors, and biocides to prevent bacterial contamination.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why is alkaline degreasing necessary before applying conversion coatings to automotive steel?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Automotive sheet steel arrives at the assembly plant coated in protective rust-preventive oils, stamping residues, and drawing lubricants. If conversion coatings (like zinc phosphate) are applied directly to these surfaces, the oil acts as a hydrophobic barrier that prevents the acidic phosphating solution from reacting with the steel surface. This results in patchy, non-adherent conversion layers. Alkaline degreasing removes all organic contaminants by emulsification and saponification, leaving a high-energy, clean surface for uniform passivating coating deposition.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What are the key requirements for thermal interface adhesives in electric vehicle (EV) battery packs?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Thermal interface adhesives in EV battery packs must perform three critical roles: structural bonding of cells to the cooling plate, high thermal conductivity (typically 1.5 to 3.0 W/mK) to dissipate heat from the battery cells, and high dielectric strength to ensure electrical isolation between modules. These formulations are typically based on silicone or polyurethane resins filled with high loadings of thermally conductive, electrically insulating mineral particles (such as alumina or aluminum trihydroxide).</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does moisture-cure polyurethane seam sealer differ from toughened epoxy structural adhesive?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Toughened epoxy structural adhesives are high-strength, high-modulus materials designed to transfer load and reinforce joint structural integrity, yielding rigid bonds. Moisture-cure polyurethane seam sealers, in contrast, are low-modulus, highly elastomeric materials designed specifically to seal joints against water, air, and dust, absorbing vibration and accommodating differential thermal expansion without cracking or losing adhesion.</div>
        </details>
